# TempTime environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in real values. `.env.local` is
# git-ignored; this template is the only env file that is ever committed.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every value below comes from one Supabase project (Settings -> API), except
# CRON_SECRET, which you invent yourself.

# Take both keys from the "Publishable and secret API keys" tab, not from the
# "Legacy anon, service_role API keys" tab beside it. The roles map one to one
# — publishable replaces anon, secret replaces service_role — and the legacy
# pair is on its way out.

# --- Supabase: public, shipped to the browser -------------------------------
# Safe to expose. The publishable key is constrained by Row Level Security;
# with RLS in place it can read a room's settings and participant list and
# nothing else. Starts with `sb_publishable_`.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# --- Supabase: server only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_ ------------------------------
# The secret key bypasses RLS entirely. It is the only credential that can read
# `submissions`, which holds every member's individual mask. It must never
# appear in a client component or a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name. Starts with `sb_secret_`
# and is shown once, when it is created.

SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# The HS256 secret shared with Supabase. It signs the room token issued when
# someone joins, and every authenticated route verifies against it. New projects
# sign with asymmetric keys by default, so this may require enabling the legacy
# JWT secret under Settings -> API.
#
# REQUIRED. Leaving it empty does not degrade anything gracefully: joining a
# room returns 500, because the token cannot be signed in the first place.
# Measured, not assumed — an earlier version of this comment claimed Realtime
# would fall back to polling, and that is simply not what happens.
#
# Sharing the secret with Supabase is what lets Realtime, and not only our own
# routes, verify a token we minted. Once rooms exist, do not rotate the
# project's JWT keys: the verification list holds one current and one previous
# key, another rotation pushes HS256 out, and room tokens live up to 3 months.

SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# --- Scheduled purge --------------------------------------------------------
# A value you choose. Guards the cron route that deletes expired rooms, so a
# stranger cannot trigger deletions by hitting the URL. Any long random string:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"

CRON_SECRET=
